From: koalabuttz <27229333+koalabuttz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 05:26:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: keep the script parser's state and value stacks level
 when they grow

yyparse stored the first state at yyss[1] while the first value stayed at
yyvs[0], so the state stack ran one slot ahead. When the stacks grew,
yyvsp was set from the state stack's depth, one slot too high, and every
later rule read the wrong values: the parse tree came out as garbage and
the compiler crashed on it. The stacks grow at about 200 states, which a
chain of 32 "if ... else if" branches or 200 nested parentheses reaches.

Restore bison's layout: the first state goes to yyss[0] through
yysetstate, and yynewstate increments before the store.

Checked with the parser built alone, 32-bit, under ASan: both cases crash
before and build the right tree after. May be behind upstream issue 89.
---
 src/script/scr_yacc2.cpp | 8 ++++----
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/script/scr_yacc2.cpp b/src/script/scr_yacc2.cpp
index ae07d668..79beda16 100644
--- a/src/script/scr_yacc2.cpp
+++ b/src/script/scr_yacc2.cpp
@@ -1080,7 +1080,7 @@ int yyparse()
 	yyssp = yyss;
 	yyvsp = yyvs;
 
-//	goto yysetstate;
+	goto yysetstate;
 
 	while (1)
 	{
@@ -1090,10 +1090,10 @@ int yyparse()
 yynewstate:
 		/* In all cases, when you get here, the value and location stacks
 		have just been pushed.  So pushing a state here evens the stacks.  */
-		//yyssp++; // LWSS CHANGE - move ++ below
+		yyssp++;
 
-//	yysetstate:
-		*++yyssp = yystate;
+yysetstate:
+		*yyssp = yystate;
 
 		if (yyssp >= &yyss[yystacksize - 1])
 		{
